\paragraph{Computing the ``Location'' Factors $\theta$, $R$:}
Next, we construct $
(\cdot+k,\cdot^p,k\cdot,+_2,\div_2,\operatorname{Id}_{\mathbb{R}})_{k\in \mathbb{R},p>0}
$-circuits computing $\theta$ and $R(x)$, cf.~\eqref{eq:entropy-variance-extension__primal__THEVARIABLES}. We first compute $\theta$. 

We now formally compile the circuit sketched in Figure~\ref{fig:theta_circuit}.  
For $N=1$, simply use the constant gate $\mathsf c_1$. 
For $N\ge2$, consider the DAG of subcircuits $\mathcal C_{\theta:N}\ensuremath{\stackrel{\mbox{\upshape\tiny def.}}{=}}(V,E,(g_v)_{v\in V})$, with input block $v_0$ carrying $x\in\mathbb R^d$ and designated outputs $(v_{6,j})_{j=1}^N$, where
$$
\begin{aligned}
V
&=\{v_0\}
\cup\{v_{i,j}:i\in[4],\,j\in[N]\}
\cup\{v_{5,1}\}
\cup\{v_{6,j}:j\in[N]\},\\
E
&=\{(v_0,v_{1,j}):j\in[N]\}
\cup\{(v_{i,j},v_{i+1,j}):i\in[3],\,j\in[N]\}\\
&\quad\cup\{(v_{4,j},v_{5,1}):j\in[N]\}
\cup\{(v_{4,j},v_{6,j}):j\in[N]\}
\cup\{(v_{5,1},v_{6,j}):j\in[N]\}.
\end{aligned}
$$
Assign the maps
\[
g_v=
\begin{cases}
\operatorname{Id}_{\mathbb R^d},&v=v_0,\\
\cdot-X_j,&v=v_{1,j},\\
|\cdot|,&v=v_{2,j},\\
\cdot^{-q},&v=v_{3,j},\\
\mu_j\cdot,&v=v_{4,j},\\
\operatorname{SUM}_N,&v=v_{5,1},\\
\div_2,&v=v_{6,j}.
\end{cases}
\]
Each displayed operation is understood as its scalar $
(\cdot+k,\cdot^p,k\cdot,+_2,\div_2,\operatorname{Id}_{\mathbb{R}})_{k\in \mathbb{R},p>0}
$-subcircuit; the input block expands into $d$ identity vertices with lifting channel $\Pi=I_d$ 

\footnote{Here squaring is understood on all of $\mathbb R$, and $r^{-q}$ is implemented as $1/r^q$ for $r>0$ and we choose numerical labels with $v_{4,j}<v_{5,1}$, so the final division has the prescribed argument order.} 
.

Then, on $U\ensuremath{\stackrel{\mbox{\upshape\tiny def.}}{=}}\mathbb R^d\setminus\{X_1,\ldots,X_N\}$, the designated outputs satisfy
\[
f_{\mathcal C_{\theta:N}}(x)
=\left(\frac{\mu_j|x-X_j|^{-q}}{\sum_{k=1}^N\mu_k|x-X_k|^{-q}}\right)_{j=1}^N
=\theta(x).
\]
Moreover, the sub-circuits (computing the coordinate-functions of $\theta$) of $\mathcal C_{\theta:N}$ has depth $6$, width $N$, and size $5N+1$. After scalar expansion, its size and width are $\mathcal{O}(dN)$, and its depth is $\mathcal{O}(\log d+\log N)$\footnote{For $N=1$, simply use the constant sub-circuit $\mathsf c_1(t)\ensuremath{\stackrel{\mbox{\upshape\tiny def.}}{=}}(0t)+1$.}.

A similar construction shows that $R$ can be computed by a $(\cdot+k,\cdot^p,k\cdot,+_2,\div_2,\operatorname{Id}_{\mathbb R})_{k\in\mathbb R,\,p>0}$-subcircuit of depth $\mathcal O(\log d+\log N)$, with width and size $\mathcal O(dN)$.




